# Quality artifacts are generated by `bin/dk pr`, and a branch that merges main
# must regenerate them in full regardless — the gate recomputes content hashes
# against the working tree, so an artifact carried across a merge is stale by
# construction. Merging these files by content therefore produces conflict
# markers in files that are about to be overwritten wholesale: resolution work
# that carries no information into the result.
#
# `merge=ours` keeps one side verbatim instead of interleaving them. That cannot
# mask a stale artifact. CI's verdict comes from `bin/package-hashes.py
# validate`, which recomputes every package and workspace hash from the checkout
# and compares them against the ones the artifact recorded; an artifact that was
# merged but not regenerated fails there exactly as loudly as a conflicted one,
# naming the packages that need re-validation. The re-run subsumes the
# resolution, which is why dropping the resolution costs the gate nothing.
#
# A merge driver is a *name*, not a behavior: when `merge.ours.driver` is unset
# git silently falls back to the default text merge, and this line does nothing.
# `bin/setup-git-config.sh` configures it, `bin/dk` invokes that on every run,
# and tests/test_quality_artifact_contract.py fails if the two stop agreeing —
# without which this file would look like protection while providing none.
.quality-artifacts/** merge=ours
